{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba knowledge/decisions/ADR-0427-reject-and-expire-pending-tool-approvals.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# ADR-0427 — Reject and expire pending tool approvals\n     2\t\n     3\t- Date: 2026-09-16\n     4\t- Status: Accepted — operator S8_REPAIRS_2 / WR-01 (R359 continuation)\n     5\t- Scope: pending tool approval lifetime and capacity\n     6\t- Supersedes: ADR-0423 no-expiry policy and consume-only store interface\n     7\t\n     8\t## Decision\n     9\t\n    10\tToolExec.reject(approvalId) validates the UUID and atomically deletes the pending record\n    11\twithout returning execution data or spawning. It returns true for a removed pending\n    12\trecord, false for missing/expired/consumed IDs. Caller authorization remains mandatory.\n    13\tRejection and execution consume compete for one deletion; a rejection cannot revoke an\n    14\texecution that already consumed the record. Rejected IDs cannot be replayed.\n    15\t\n    16\tEvery proposal has a digest-bound expiresAt equal to issuance time plus a fixed fifteen\n    17\tminutes (APPROVAL_TTL_MS). No renewal or client-selected lifetime is accepted. The existing\n    18\tinjected clock supplies epoch milliseconds; production uses Date.now. Execution rechecks\n    19\texpiry after its awaited atomic consume, so an expired record returned by a durable\n    20\tadapter cannot spawn. Applications must provide trustworthy clocks and private stores.\n    21\t\n    22\tThe memory store prunes expired entries before put/reject and discards expired consumes.\n    23\tThus abandoned records no longer permanently occupy its 1,000-slot bound. Idle expired\n    24\tentries may remain allocated until an operation, but are bounded and have no execution\n    25\tauthority. Durable adapters must provide mutually atomic consume/reject, enforce expiry,\n    26\tand reclaim expired capacity using the same clock domain. Restart behavior and all other\n    27\tADR-0423 digest, policy, schema, environment and caller-authentication rules remain.\n    28\t\n    29\t## Verification and compatibility\n    30\t\n    31\tTests cover 1,000 rejected and 1,000 expired records reclaiming capacity, exact expiry,\n    32\tno spawn or replay after rejection, both rejection/consumption race orders, and an expired\n    33\trecord returned by a durable adapter. A caller cannot alter the digest-bound timestamp.\n    34\tNamed mutation arms and restoration hashes are in RUN-NOTES. The pending tool-exec minor\n    35\tchangeset includes the new public timestamp and required store.reject adapter method.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"agent_message","text":"The commerce repair is fail-closed at the actual production route: provider and membership errors are caught only by the checkout handler’s 503 boundary, explicit unverified account cookies are rejected, and entitlement plus Paddle scopes both receive the verified account. The approval repair also implements one-winner deletion, digest-bound expiry, post-consume expiry recheck, capacity reclamation, and no-spawn rejection; I’m now checking its consumers and the earlier cumulative security seams for regressions or unmapped bypasses."}}
