-image.yml-218-      # Anything that must happen on the HOST before Docker is handed the build\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:219:      # context. dev-profile is why this exists: its Dockerfile builds a staged\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-220-      # public-only tree (tools/build-site-target.ts -> .site-build-public/) behind a\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:221:      # .dockerignore allowlist, so the staging step cannot move inside the Dockerfile —\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-222-      # by the time Docker runs, the context has already been filtered.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-223-      #\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-224-      # REQUIRED in every repo, not skip-if-absent. A repo with nothing to prepare ships\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-225-      # a one-line script saying so. An `if [ -f ... ]` guard here would make \"no\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-226-      # preparation needed\" and \"the script is missing\" identical at a glance, and the\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-227-      # failure mode of the second is an image built from the wrong tree that passes\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-228-      # every gate above it.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-229-      - name: prepare build context\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-230-        run: bash deploy/prepare-build.sh\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-231-\n--\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-239-          # `create_credentials_file: false` is the mechanical guarantee; .dockerignore\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-240-          # entries are the belt, this is the braces.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-241-          create_credentials_file: false\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-242-\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-243-      # `GCP_REGISTRY_HOST` is the host `docker login` sends the access token to, and\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-244-      # `GCP_REGISTRY` is the host the image is actually pushed to. They are two\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-245-      # independent repository variables that only happen to agree, and nothing checked\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-246-      # that they did — so a principal who can write repository variables but cannot read\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-247-      # secrets (a `repo`-scoped PAT, an org owner setting an org-level var) could point\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-248-      # the login at a host they control and receive a live GCP access token for the\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:249:      # image-publisher SA. The push to GCP_REGISTRY then fails and the run goes red,\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-250-      # after the credential has already left. `create_credentials_file: false` does not\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-251-      # help: it stops the credential reaching a Docker CONTEXT, not a login to the wrong\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-252-      # host.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-253-      #\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-254-      # Asserted rather than derived because `docker login` takes a host, not a\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-255-      # host-plus-path, so GCP_REGISTRY cannot simply be passed through. The pair check is\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-256-      # the property that matters: the token only ever goes to the host the image goes to.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-257-      - name: assert the login host is the registry's own host\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-258-        env:\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-259-          REGISTRY: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}\n--\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-270-          esac\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-271-\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-272-      - uses: docker/login-action@dbcb813823bdd20940b903addbd779551569679f # v4.6.0\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-273-        with:\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-274-          registry: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY_HOST }}\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-275-          username: oauth2accesstoken\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-276-          password: ${{ steps.auth.outputs.access_token }}\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-277-\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-278-      - uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@37fe631027851001ddb9b187196cc803df7f5f0e # v4.3.0\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-279-\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:280:      # CORE-73: a workflow_dispatch re-run of an already-published sha used to go RED at\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:281:      # push time — the rebuild is not byte-reproducible, so the same :sha tag arrives\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:282:      # with a new manifest and Artifact Registry's tag immutability refuses it. A red\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:283:      # that reads as a regression while saying only \"this was already published\". The\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-284-      # registry's existing digest IS the truth for that sha, so a re-run re-emits it and\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-285-      # skips the build instead of failing. It is the truth about WHAT the bytes are —\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-286-      # not about whether they were signed or scanned: the run that pushed may have died\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-287-      # between the push and either claim, so the evidence chain below keys on the\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-288-      # resolved digest (see `resolve digest`) and re-earns both on every run.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-289-      #\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:290:      # Three outcomes, fail-closed: the tag resolves -> published=true plus its digest;\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:291:      # the registry says the tag/repo does not exist -> published=false, build proceeds;\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-292-      # anything else (auth, network, parse) -> red. \"Cannot tell\" must never build —\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:293:      # guessing \"not published\" here would recreate the immutability red this exists to\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-294-      # remove, one step later and with less context. `imagetools inspect` reads through\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-295-      # the docker login above and returns the same index digest buildx emitted when it\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-296-      # pushed, which is what every deploy and rollback consumes.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:297:      - name: already published at this sha?\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-298-        id: preflight\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-299-        env:\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-300-          IMAGE: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}/${{ matrix.service }}\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:301:          TAG: ${{ github.sha }}\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-302-        run: |\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-303-          set -euo pipefail\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-304-          if out=\"$(docker buildx imagetools inspect \"${IMAGE}:${TAG}\" --format '{{json .Manifest}}' 2>preflight-err.txt)\"; then\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-305-            digest=\"$(printf '%s' \"$out\" | jq -r '.digest // empty')\"\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-306-            case \"$digest\" in\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-307-              sha256:?*) ;;\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-308-              *) echo \"::error::registry answered for ${IMAGE}:${TAG} but returned no parseable digest\"; exit 1 ;;\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-309-            esac\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:310:            printf 'published=true\\ndigest=%s\\n' \"$digest\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:311:            echo \"::notice title=already published (CORE-73)::${IMAGE}:${TAG}@${digest} — build skipped, digest re-emitted\"\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-312-          elif grep -qiE 'not found|NAME_UNKNOWN|MANIFEST_UNKNOWN' preflight-err.txt; then\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:313:            printf 'published=false\\n' >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-314-          else\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:315:            echo \"::error::cannot determine whether ${IMAGE}:${TAG} is already published — refusing to guess\"\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-316-            cat preflight-err.txt >&2\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-317-            exit 1\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-318-          fi\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-319-\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:320:      # No `latest`. Doc 07 §4: human-readable tags may carry the full SHA, but nothing\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:321:      # in a deploy or a rollback ever resolves a tag — they consume the digest this step\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:322:      # emits. A floating tag in a rollback path is how you roll back to the thing you\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-323-      # were trying to escape.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-324-      #\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:325:      # Skipped when preflight found the tag (CORE-73). The evidence chain below does NOT\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-326-      # skip with it: sign, SBOM, scan, manifest, and the evidence upload key on the\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-327-      # resolved digest, not on this step — evidence follows the digest, whichever run\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-328-      # pushed it (see `resolve digest`).\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-329-      - name: build and push by digest\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-330-        id: build\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:331:        if: steps.preflight.outputs.published != 'true'\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-332-        uses: docker/build-push-action@53b7df96c91f9c12dcc8a07bcb9ccacbed38856a # v7.3.0\n.github/workflows/p