ned, then went red on a CRITICAL — \"Re-run failed jobs\" published\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-354-      # the blocked digest green, with production's cosign verify passing because run 1\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-355-      # genuinely signed; and a sign that died after the push, whose digest no later run\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-356-      # could ever sign — every re-run entered the skip path and skipped sign with it.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-357-      #\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-358-      # This step resolves THE digest — the build's, or the one preflight read back —\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-359-      # and everything below keys on its existence: `always() && steps.image.outputs.digest != ''`.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-360-      # A skipped step's outputs are empty strings, so a failed build still skips the\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-361-      # chain and a red preflight still reds the job: fail-closed unchanged. On the skip\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-362-      # path the run re-EARNS the claims instead of inheriting them: cosign sign is\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-363-      # idempotent (one more Rekor entry, same verification), the SBOM and the scan read\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-364-      # the digest from the registry by ref — no rebuild — and a repo that set\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:365:      # TRIVY_EXIT_CODE=1 keeps its gate: a re-run of a scan-blocked publish re-scans\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-366-      # and goes red again. The deliberate cost: with TRIVY_EXIT_CODE=1, a re-dispatch\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:367:      # of an old green sha can go red on a CVE published since — that is the repo's own\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-368-      # policy speaking, not a liveness failure; the unset-default ('0') re-dispatch\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-369-      # stays green and keeps the liveness-probe property.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-370-      - name: resolve digest\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-371-        id: image\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-372-        env:\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-373-          # Whichever path ran: the build's digest, or the one preflight read back from\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-374-          # the registry (CORE-73). Exactly one is ever non-empty.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-375-          DIGEST: ${{ steps.build.outputs.digest || steps.preflight.outputs.digest }}\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-376-        run: |\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-377-          set -euo pipefail\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-378-          printf 'digest=%s\\n' \"$DIGEST\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-379-\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:380:      # doc 07 §4 asks for provenance on the published digest. This was\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:381:      # `actions/attest-build-provenance`, and on three of the four repos it failed AFTER\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:382:      # the push: the GitHub attestations API is plan-gated for private repositories, so\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-383-      # the step went red with the digest already in the registry, `collect` was skipped,\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:384:      # and `digests` never published — a good build made unshippable by a billing tier.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-385-      # Operator ruling R44 (2026-08-25): sign with Sigstore directly, no GitHub API in\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-386-      # the path.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-387-      #\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-388-      # Keyless. cosign trades this job's OIDC token for a short-lived Fulcio certificate\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-389-      # whose subject IS this workflow file at this ref, signs the digest, and records the\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-390-      # signature in the public Rekor log. There is no key to hold, rotate, or leak, and\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-391-      # the verifier — deploy-production.yml, the one place these digests become\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-392-      # production revisions — checks an identity STRING, not a key it has to fetch: a\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-393-      # digest that this workflow at refs/heads/main did not sign is refused there before\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-394-      # any revision exists. A key-based alternative (a KMS key) would trade that for new\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-395-      # infra, IAM on two identities, and a verifier that must hold a credential.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-396-      #\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-397-      # Residual, stated rather than implied: the Rekor entry is public and names this\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-398-      # repository, this workflow path, the commit, and the image reference (registry\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-399-      # host, GCP project, service). Nothing secret — but a private repo's name and its\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-400-      # registry path become discoverable. Accepted by the ruling.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-401-      #\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml:402:      # Signed by DIGEST: a tag is a pointer, and signing the pointer proves nothing about\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-403-      # what it points at today. `--yes` is the non-interactive consent to the Rekor\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-404-      # upload. The pinned `cosign-release` must match deploy-production.yml's — the two\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-405-      # are one number in two files, and the template test holds them equal.\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-406-      - uses: sigstore/cosign-installer@6f9f17788090df1f26f669e9d70d6ae9567deba6 # v4.1.2\n.github/workflows/publish-image.yml-