{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'head -n 220 .github/workflows/publish-image.yml'","aggregated_output":"# T28 reference: image publication (doc 07 §3 + §4).\n#\n# COPY THIS FILE VERBATIM into each app repo as `.github/workflows/publish-image.yml`.\n# Everything repo-specific is a variable or lives in `deploy/services.json`, so the four\n# copies stay byte-identical and drift is detectable by comparing hashes. If you find\n# yourself editing the body for one repo, that is a bug in this template — fix it here.\n#\n# What it is NOT: a deployer. Publishing and deploying are separate identities (doc 07\n# §2) and separate workflows on purpose. This job may write to Artifact Registry and\n# nothing else; it holds no Cloud Run permission at all.\n\nname: publish image\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n  workflow_dispatch:\n    inputs:\n      service:\n        description: \"Single service key to publish; default is every service this repo owns\"\n        required: false\n        type: string\n  workflow_call:\n    inputs:\n      service:\n        required: false\n        type: string\n    outputs:\n      digests:\n        description: JSON object mapping service key to the published image digest\n        value: ${{ jobs.collect.outputs.digests }}\n\n# doc 07 §1: never two publishes of the same ref at once — the second would race the\n# first's provenance attestation onto a digest it did not build.\nconcurrency:\n  group: publish-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: false\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  # Which services does this push actually change? `watched_paths` in the canonical\n  # manifest answers exactly that question (doc 07 §4), so it is the matrix source —\n  # never a hand-maintained list that drifts from the manifest the review approved.\n  select:\n    runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LINUX || 'ubuntu-latest' }}\n    outputs:\n      matrix: ${{ steps.pick.outputs.matrix }}\n      any: ${{ steps.pick.outputs.any }}\n    steps:\n      # An unset REPOSITORY VARIABLE interpolates to the empty string. It does not fail, and\n      # GitHub — not this template — owns what \"empty\" then means, so an absent variable is a\n      # value chosen by someone who is not in the room.\n      #\n      # `BUN_VERSION_FILE` is the one that proves this is not hygiene. Read at the pinned\n      # setup-bun SHA (`0c5077e5`, `action.yml`: neither `bun-version` nor `bun-version-file`\n      # is required, the latter defaults to null; `src/action.ts:158`: \"If no version\n      # specified, default is 'latest'\"). So an empty value does not error — it installs the\n      # newest Bun, the repo's pin silently stops binding, and the run is green. Same shape\n      # as the missing dependency install: a success nobody had to produce.\n      #\n      # Asserted for every variable the job reads rather than only the ones argued to be\n      # dangerous, because deciding which ones self-report is exactly the reasoning that\n      # produced the gap. Each job lists its own; a job that stops reading one drops it here\n      # in the same edit.\n      #\n      # First step in the job — before checkout, before `setup-bun`, before any credential.\n      # A misconfigured repo costs one red run and nothing else.\n      - name: assert the repository variables are set\n        env:\n          BUN_VERSION_FILE: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          missing=\"\"\n          for v in BUN_VERSION_FILE; do\n            [ -n \"${!v:-}\" ] || missing=\"$missing $v\"\n          done\n          if [ -n \"$missing\" ]; then\n            echo \"::error::unset repository variables:$missing\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n\n      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1\n        with:\n          fetch-depth: 0\n\n      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@0c5077e51419868618aeaa5fe8019c62421857d6 # v2.2.0\n        with:\n          bun-version-file: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n\n      # Every `bun run deploy/*.ts` in this pipeline resolves this repo's dependencies, and\n      # until 2026-08-21 nothing installed them. Bun's auto-install covered that silently:\n      # it fetches from the registry against package.json RANGES, not `bun.lock`, which\n      # made the deploy path the only place in these repos where the lockfile did not bind.\n      # Every other workflow in all four repos runs `bun install --frozen-lockfile`; the\n      # T28 template was the exception, and that was an oversight, not a decision.\n      #\n      # gridwork is the one repo with nothing to install: it has no root `package.json`\n      # (its Bun project is `site/`) and its `deploy/*.ts` are deliberately\n      # dependency-free. The `exit 1` arm is what keeps that exemption honest — a repo that\n      # grows a package import without a root lockfile fails here instead of quietly\n      # auto-installing. `node:` and `bun:` specifiers are builtins, installed from nowhere.\n      #\n      # This step is byte-identical in all four workflows. Copy it, do not adapt it.\n      - name: install the deploy scripts' dependencies\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          if [ -f bun.lock ]; then\n            bun install --frozen-lockfile\n          elif grep -hoE 'from \"[^\"]+\"' deploy/*.ts | grep -qvE 'from \"(\\.|node:|bun:)'; then\n            echo \"::error::deploy/*.ts imports a package but this repo has no root bun.lock\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n\n      - id: pick\n        env:\n          ONLY: ${{ inputs.service }}\n          BEFORE: ${{ github.event.before }}\n        run: bun run deploy/select-services.ts >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n\n  publish:\n    needs: select\n    if: needs.select.outputs.any == 'true'\n    runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LINUX || 'ubuntu-latest' }}\n    timeout-minutes: 45\n    strategy:\n      fail-fast: false\n      matrix:\n        service: ${{ fromJSON(needs.select.outputs.matrix) }}\n    permissions:\n      contents: read\n      # doc 07 §1 rule 3: OIDC/WIF only, never a JSON key. The same token is what cosign\n      # trades for its signing certificate below — one grant, two consumers, no key anywhere.\n      # `attestations: write` is gone with the attestation API (see \"sign the digest\").\n      id-token: write\n    steps:\n      # doc 07 §3: a fork PR gets nothing. This workflow is push/dispatch-only so a fork\n      # cannot reach it, but say so out loud rather than relying on the trigger list\n      # staying that way — the failure mode of a widened trigger is silent credential\n      # exposure, and this check costs one step.\n      - name: refuse a fork ref\n        if: github.event.pull_request.head.repo.fork == true\n        run: |\n          echo \"::error::fork refs never publish images (doc 07 §3)\"\n          exit 1\n\n      # See the `select` job for why an unset repository variable is a vendor-chosen value\n      # rather than a failure. This job reads five; it asserts all five.\n      - name: assert the repository variables are set\n        env:\n          BUN_VERSION_FILE: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n          GCP_IMAGE_PUBLISHER_SA: ${{ vars.GCP_IMAGE_PUBLISHER_SA }}\n          GCP_REGISTRY: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY }}\n          GCP_REGISTRY_HOST: ${{ vars.GCP_REGISTRY_HOST }}\n          GCP_WIF_PROVIDER: ${{ vars.GCP_WIF_PROVIDER }}\n          NODE_VERSION_FILE: ${{ vars.NODE_VERSION_FILE }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          missing=\"\"\n          for v in BUN_VERSION_FILE GCP_IMAGE_PUBLISHER_SA GCP_REGISTRY GCP_REGISTRY_HOST GCP_WIF_PROVIDER NODE_VERSION_FILE; do\n            [ -n \"${!v:-}\" ] || missing=\"$missing $v\"\n          done\n          if [ -n \"$missing\" ]; then\n            echo \"::error::unset repository variables:$missing\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n\n      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1\n\n      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@0c5077e51419868618aeaa5fe8019c62421857d6 # v2.2.0\n        with:\n          bun-version-file: ${{ vars.BUN_VERSION_FILE }}\n\n      # CAISSON-209 (2026-08-25, caisson run 32904702863): `deploy/gates.sh` shells Node\n      # tools — dependency-cruiser's floor is ^22||^24||>=26 — and a Bun-only job runs\n      # Node SHIMS on whatever Node the runner image ships (20.20.0 that day). With no\n      # pin there was nothing to drift and nothing to report until a tool raised its\n      # floor. Same discipline as BUN_VERSION_FILE: the version lives in a repo-owned pin\n      # file named by a repository variable asserted above — never a literal here that\n      # drifts per repo — and an EMPTY file input falls back to the same system Node this\n      # exists to escape, which is why the variable is in the assertion.\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@820762786026740c76f36085b0efc47a31fe5020 # v7.0.0\n        with:\n          node-version-file: ${{ vars.NODE_VERSION_FILE }}\n\n      # Same step as the `select` job above — see there for why it is not an unconditional\n      # `bun install` and what the `exit 1` arm catches. The bodies must not drift.\n      - name: install the deploy scripts' dependencies\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          if [ -f bun.lock ]; then\n            bun install --frozen-lockfile\n          elif grep -hoE 'from \"[^\"]+\"' deploy/*.ts | grep -qvE 'from \"(\\.|node:|bun:)'; then\n            echo \"::error::deploy/*.ts imports a package but this repo has no root bun.lock\"\n            exit 1\n          fi\n\n      - name: resolve build inputs\n        id: svc\n        env:\n          SERVICE: ${{ matrix.service }}\n        run: bun run deploy/service-inputs.ts >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n\n      # doc 07 §3: lint, typecheck, tests, and an image that builds — before anything is\n      # pushed anywhere. A digest that was never tested is worse than no digest, because\n      # the rollback ladder will happily promote it.\n      #\n      # The commands live in the repo's own `deploy/gates.sh`, not here. They were literal\n      # `bun run lint/typecheck/test` until the gridwork adoption proved that wrong: that\n      # repo has no root package.json, pins Bun at site/package.json, and runs its lint\n      # through ./tools/lint.sh. Four repos do not share a script vocabulary, and a\n      # template that assumes they do forces exactly the per-repo body edit this design\n      # exists to prevent. `gates.sh` must exit non-zero when any gate fails.\n      - name: gates\n        run: bash deploy/gates.sh\n\n      # Anything that must happen on the HOST before Docker is handed the build\n      # context. dev-profile is why this exists: its Dockerfile builds a staged\n      # public-only tree (tools/build-site-target.ts -> .site-build-public/) behind a\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
